response:
To further optimize efficiency for handling a large amount of text, you may want to consider using memory-mapped files instead of reading the entire text into memory at once. This allows you to access the data as if it were loaded into memory without actually consuming all of your system's RAM. Here's how you can modify the code above to use memory-mapped files:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1Path, text2Path)
    % Read text files using memory mapping
    map1 = mmap('r', text1Path, 'clr');
    map2 = mmap('r', text2Path, 'clr');

    % Convert text to lowercase and split into words
    words1 = lower(strsplit(map1(:)));
    words2 = lower(strsplit(map2(:)));

    % Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
    uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);

    % Close memory maps
    close(map1);
    close(map2);
end
```

In this function, `text1Path` and `text2Path` are the paths to the input text files. The function returns a cell array containing the unique words found in `text1` but not in `text2`. Note that we pass the 'clr' option to `mmap()` to enable transparent cross-platform line endings conversion between DOS/Windows (CRLF) and Unix/Linux (LF).

This implementation can significantly reduce the memory footprint of your program since only a small portion of the text is loaded into memory at any given time. It also has the added benefit of allowing you to seek directly to specific positions within the mapped files, which can improve performance when searching for specific patterns or substrings.

